<spell-notebook "input usage">
	<header level="1">
		Input
		<header>An input is a field used to elicit a response from a user.</>
	</>
	<input-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#forms-and-validation">Forms and validation</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  forms, validation, events, keyboard, accessibility, frameworks. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-input>` is a single-line field; for a multi-line one, [`<ui-textarea>`](/components/ui/ui-textarea.html) shares its sheet and
			most attributes.

			- `type` passes through to the native input (`file` also gets Fomantic's file look).
			- `icon` draws an icon inside the field, at the end (or the start, with `icon-position="left"`).
			- `label` joins a label; `labeled` places it (`right`, `corner`, `left corner`). The `label` slot takes markup: a
			  `<ui-label appearance="basic">`, a `<ui-dropdown>`.
			- `<ui-button>`s (or a `<ui-dropdown button>`) in the `action` slot join the field; `action="left"` puts them
			  first.
			- `state` tints the field: `error`, `info`, `success`, `warning`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Forms and validation">
		<markdown>
			An input is a real form control: put it inside a `<form>` and its `name` / `value` is submitted with it (a
			`file` input submits one entry per chosen file).

			- As with a native input, the `value` ATTRIBUTE is the starting value and the `value` PROPERTY the live one;
			  `form.reset()` goes back to the attribute, and a disabled `<fieldset>` disables it.
			- `readonly` keeps the value in the submission, `disabled` leaves it out.
			- Constraint attributes (`required`, `pattern`, `min`, `max`, `step`, `minlength`, `maxlength`) validate natively;
			  the `rules` property adds Fomantic's rules on top (`"email"`, `["notEmpty", "minLength[6]"]`).
			- The field only shows as invalid once the user has interacted with it, as `:user-invalid` does.
			- **Enter** submits its form.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-input` fires on every keystroke, with `event.detail.value`.
			- `ui-changed` fires when the user commits (blur, **Enter**, picking a file).
			- Setting `value` from a `ui-input` handler wins over what was typed, so a handler can reformat or reject input.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const input = document.querySelector("ui-input[name=email]")
			input.rules = ["notEmpty", "email"]
			input.addEventListener("ui-input", (event) => {
			  input.value = event.detail.value.toLowerCase()
			})
			input.addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.value, input.validity.valid))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			The shadow root holds a real `<input>`, so the browser does the keyboard work.

			- **Tab** / **Shift+Tab** moves focus to and from the field; the host delegates focus, so it is never a tab stop
			  of its own, and clicking the host focuses the control.
			- **Enter** submits its form (clicking the form's first submit button), as a native field does.
			- Buttons in the `action` slot are tab stops of their own, after (or before) the field.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Typing, selection, autofill and the on-screen keyboard come from the browser's own control.
			- Name the field with a `<label for>` pointing at the host's `id` (as in a form) or an `aria-label` on the host;
			  either becomes the control's accessible name. A `placeholder` is not a label.
			- A field that fails validation after the user has touched it gets `aria-invalid="true"`; a `<ui-form>` marks
			  failing fields the same way. `loading` sets `aria-busy`.
			- The `icon` and the `corner` label are decoration; they are hidden from assistive technology, so don't put
			  meaning in them alone.
			- If the element fails to render, its native fallback keeps the same box with a plain native control:
			  it still submits, validates its native constraints and fires `ui-input` / `ui-changed`. It drops the
			  icon glyph, the `icon`, `label` and `action` slots, corner labels, `rules`, the invalid state, reset
			  to the starting value, **Enter** to submit,
			  and naming through `<label for>` (only `aria-label` or `placeholder` name the control).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>
			Set `rules` as a property and listen for `ui-input` / `ui-changed`. Bind `value` as a property to control the
			field.
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-input name="email" type="email" value={email} rules={["email"]} onui-input={(e) => setEmail(e.detail.value)} />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-input name="email" type="email" :value="email" :rules="['email']" @ui-input="(e) => (email = e.detail.value)" />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-input
			  name="email"
			  type="email"
			  value={email()}
			  rules={["email"]}
			  on-ui-typed={(e) => setEmail(e.detail.value)}
			/>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
